-- ==============================================================================
-- gatwd Benchmark: Ackermann A(3, n) — interpreter throughput & peak-memory probe
-- ==============================================================================
-- Reuses the tour's Ackermann clauses (examples/tour/23_ackermann.gatwd) as a
-- deliberately allocation-heavy workload. It is written as clause definitions, so each
-- call goes through a curried wrapper and a hidden match, and the nested call is an
-- argument — deep recursion stresses the interpreter's allocation and scoping paths.
--
-- Why A(3, n): it stays on the native int64 fast path (A(3, n) = 2^(n+3) - 3), so
-- this measures *interpreter overhead*, not big.Int arithmetic. Call count — and
-- thus wall-clock — grows ~4x per step, making `n` a single calibration knob:
--
--   A(3,3) ~0.04s   A(3,4) ~0.3s   A(3,5) ~1.2s   A(3,6) ~5s
--
-- A(3,6) is the default: a multi-second run whose peak RSS stays flat (~18 MB),
-- confirming the workload is high-churn, not leaking. Bump to A(3,7) for a longer
-- run (expect ~20s). Run via `benchmarks/run_ackermann.sh` for wall + peak RSS.
--
-- Each line self-asserts the closed-form result, so a wrong answer fails loudly
-- rather than silently skewing the timing.
-- ==============================================================================

def ackermann 0 n = n + 1
def ackermann m 0 = ackermann (m - 1) 1
def ackermann m n = ackermann (m - 1) (ackermann m (n - 1))

-- Hyperoperation-3 ramp: A(3, n) = 2^(n+3) - 3. Cheap arms validate correctness;
-- the final A(3, 6) arm dominates runtime.
ackermann 3 0 |> assert 5
ackermann 3 1 |> assert 13
ackermann 3 2 |> assert 29
ackermann 3 3 |> assert 61
ackermann 3 4 |> assert 125
ackermann 3 5 |> assert 253
ackermann 3 6 |> assert 509
